/* English plan reference content, lifted verbatim from the original tracker. */
var ENG_REF = {
"overview": "<div class=\"card warn\">\n    <h3>First: you are not bad at English, and you are not bad at learning</h3>\n    <p>You did high school and college in Hong Kong and you scored <strong>Listening 9, Reading 9</strong>. Those are strong scores. You absorbed a lot of English — that part worked.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:10px;\">Your 9/9/7/6 split is <strong>the standard Hong Kong profile</strong>, and it's not a coincidence. HK English education is built around reading comprehension, grammar rules, and exam papers. Speaking gets almost no real practice, and when it does it's a scripted presentation, not a conversation. So you finished school with a big English database and almost no practice retrieving from it in real time. That's exactly what a 9/9/7/6 looks like.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:10px;\"><strong>\"I suck at study\" is probably the wrong conclusion.</strong> What you're bad at is the HK method — memorise, sit still, get tested. That method is genuinely poor at producing speakers, and disliking it says very little about you. Nothing in this plan is that method. There is no textbook, no rule memorisation, no exam papers to grind. It's closer to gym training than to school: short daily reps, out loud, and you track what you did rather than what you know.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card warn\">\n    <h3>Straight talk about the target</h3>\n    <p><strong>10 in all four bands is very rare</strong>, and S6 → S10 in 8 weeks is not a fair bet. What <em>is</em> very achievable: <strong>Writing 8–9, Speaking 8–9</strong>, which clears every immigration threshold that matters (CLB 9 = all 9s). Aim for 9s. If a 10 happens, good.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:10px;\">Don't book the test yet. Book at the end of Week 5, for a date in Week 8 or 9 — commit only once you can see the improvement yourself.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"grid2\">\n    <div class=\"card\">\n      <h3>Why you're stuck at 6–7 — the Cantonese version</h3>\n      <ul class=\"plain\">\n        <li><strong>Cantonese doesn't mark grammar the way English does.</strong> No articles, no plural -s, no verb tense. You're not forgetting rules — your brain's default operating system doesn't have those slots. See the <em>Cantonese Traps</em> tab.</li>\n        <li><strong>Half your \"grammar errors\" in speaking are actually pronunciation.</strong> Cantonese has no final consonant clusters, so <em>books</em> comes out as <em>book</em> and <em>worked</em> as <em>work</em> — even when you know the rule perfectly. The rater hears a grammar mistake. This is probably the single most fixable thing on your list.</li>\n        <li><strong>Rhythm.</strong> Cantonese gives every syllable equal weight and a tone. English squashes unstressed syllables into \"uh\". That mismatch is why you sound choppy to them, and why fast native speech sounds like mush to you. Both problems, one cause.</li>\n        <li><strong>You translate.</strong> Cantonese → English conversion costs 2–3 seconds a sentence. That gap is the hesitation the rater scores down.</li>\n        <li><strong>You've never heard yourself.</strong> The fix is mechanical, not clever: record, transcribe, correct.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"card\">\n      <h3>The three habits that do 80% of the work</h3>\n      <ul class=\"plain\">\n        <li><strong>1. Shadowing (20 min/day).</strong> Play 2 minutes of native audio and speak <em>at the same time</em>, copying rhythm and intonation. This is the single fastest fix for sounding hesitant. Non-negotiable, every day.</li>\n        <li><strong>2. Record → transcribe → correct (30 min/day).</strong> Answer a task on your phone. Type out exactly what you said, mistakes included. Fix it. Re-record. Painful, and it works faster than anything else.</li>\n        <li><strong>3. Error log (10 min/day).</strong> Every mistake goes in the log tab. Anything that appears 3+ times is your real weakness — drill that, ignore the rest.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Your daily 100 minutes</h3>\n    <table>\n      <tr><th>Block</th><th>Time</th><th>What</th></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Shadow</strong></td><td>20 min</td><td>2-min clip × 5 passes. Same clip all week — you want mastery, not variety. Can be done walking.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Sound</strong></td><td>10 min</td><td>Cantonese-specific drills: final consonants, -s / -ed endings, stress-timing. See the <em>Pronunciation</em> tab.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Speak</strong></td><td>30 min</td><td>2 CELPIP tasks. Record → transcribe → correct → re-record.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Write</strong></td><td>25 min</td><td>One timed task. Self-edit against the hit list before checking with AI.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Log</strong></td><td>10 min</td><td>Add today's errors. Re-read the whole log out loud.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Soak</strong></td><td>10+ min</td><td>Podcast on commute or while cooking. Free time, doesn't count against the 95.</td></tr>\n    </table>\n    <div class=\"tip\"><strong>Bad day protocol:</strong> shadow 10 min, one speaking task 15, error log 5. That's it, and it counts as a full day. Never zero — the streak is worth more than any single good session. Missing two days in a row is the actual failure mode, not doing a short day.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Rules for someone who hates studying</h3>\n    <ul class=\"plain\">\n      <li><strong>Never sit at a desk for the shadowing.</strong> Do it walking, on the commute, washing dishes, in the car. It is mimicry, not study, and it's 20 of your 95 minutes.</li>\n      <li><strong>Don't read about grammar.</strong> Not one page. You already did 12 years of that in HK and it produced a 6 in speaking. Your only grammar input is your own error log — errors you personally made, in sentences about your own life.</li>\n      <li><strong>Track reps, not knowledge.</strong> The checkboxes here measure whether you showed up, not whether you understood. That's deliberate. Showing up 50 days out of 56 wins regardless of how any single day felt.</li>\n      <li><strong>Bad sessions still count.</strong> A recording where you stammered for 60 seconds moved you forward more than a day of reading rules. Tick the box.</li>\n      <li><strong>Two-week rule.</strong> Judge the plan at the end of Week 2, not on day 3. The first ten days feel useless for everyone.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"grid2\">\n    <div class=\"card\">\n      <h3>Tools &amp; what to actually pay for</h3>\n      <table>\n        <tr><th>Thing</th><th>Cost</th><th>Verdict</th></tr>\n        <tr><td>CELPIP free sample test</td><td>Free</td><td>Do it in Week 1. Essential.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Official CELPIP practice tests</td><td>~$25–70 CAD</td><td><strong>Buy.</strong> Best value on this list — the real interface and real question style.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>iTalki / Preply tutor</td><td>~$15–25/hr</td><td><strong>Worth it from Week 3.</strong> 2×/week ≈ $130–200/month. Biggest single lever on speaking. A community tutor at $12–15 is fine — you need a talking partner, not a linguist.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Me (Claude), as your daily marker</td><td>Free to you</td><td>Paste writing → ask for a CELPIP band + top 3 errors. Paste your speaking transcript → same. Do this daily.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Language exchange (Tandem, HelloTalk)</td><td>Free</td><td>Good substitute if the tutor budget is tight.</td></tr>\n      </table>\n      <div class=\"tip\">Minimum spend that still works: ~$40 for official practice tests, everything else free. Recommended: that plus 2 tutor sessions/week for Weeks 3–8 (~$250 total).</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"card\">\n      <h3>Shadowing sources (Canadian / neutral North American)</h3>\n      <ul class=\"plain\">\n        <li><strong>CBC Front Burner</strong> — Canadian accent, clear, ~20 min. Best single pick for CELPIP.</li>\n        <li><strong>TED Talks</strong> — slow, well-structured, transcripts provided. Great for Weeks 1–2.</li>\n        <li><strong>Lex Fridman / Acquired</strong> — long-form tech talk. Exactly the register you need at work.</li>\n        <li><strong>Any standup or all-hands recording from your own company</strong> — if you can get one, this is the highest-value material you will ever shadow, because it's the actual vocabulary you're failing to catch.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"tip\">Always shadow <em>with</em> a transcript. Listening blind is a different (easier) exercise and won't fix your pronunciation.<br><br><strong>Retune your model.</strong> HK schooling gave you a British-ish / HK English target. CELPIP and your coworkers are North American. Different vowels, flapped <em>t</em> (<em>water</em> → \"wadder\"), rhotic <em>r</em>. Pick Canadian or US sources only for the next 8 weeks and don't mix.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>How to know it's working</h3>\n    <table>\n      <tr><th>Checkpoint</th><th>What should be true</th></tr>\n      <tr><td>End of Week 2</td><td>You can start any speaking task within 2 seconds of the beep, using a template. Quality still rough — that's fine.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>End of Week 4</td><td>Your transcripts have fewer than ~8 grammar errors per 60-second answer. You fill 55+ of the 60 seconds.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>End of Week 6</td><td>Writing consistently 150–200 words in time, with 2+ minutes left to proofread. Speaking feels boring rather than scary.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>End of Week 8</td><td>Practice test scores 8–9 in writing and speaking. Book the real thing.</td></tr>\n    </table>\n  </div>",
"speaking": "<div class=\"card warn\">\n    <h3>How to use these</h3>\n    <p>These are <strong>scaffolds, not scripts</strong>. In Weeks 1–3 use them word-for-word — that's the point, it frees your brain to think about content instead of sentence shape. From Week 4, start varying the openers so you don't sound memorised. By Week 7 they should be invisible.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:8px;\"><strong>Universal rule:</strong> never stop talking. Silence is the harshest penalty in CELPIP speaking. If you blank, say <span class=\"frame\">\"Let me put it another way —\"</span> or <span class=\"frame\">\"The thing is,\"</span> and keep going. A rambling answer scores far better than a clean answer with three long pauses.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <div class=\"tpl\">\n      <h4>Task 1 — Giving Advice</h4>\n      <div class=\"meta\">30s prep · 90s speaking · you're talking TO a person</div>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Hey [name], I heard about ___, and I've got a few thoughts.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">The first thing I'd suggest is ___, because ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Another option would be to ___. That way you could ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Honestly, if I were in your shoes, I'd go with ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Anyway, whatever you decide, let me know how it turns out.</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">90 seconds is long. You need <strong>three</strong> pieces of advice with a reason each, not two. Prep note: write only three words — one per piece of advice.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"tpl\">\n      <h4>Task 2 — Personal Experience</h4>\n      <div class=\"meta\">30s prep · 60s · this is your storytelling weakness — drill it hardest</div>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">One time that really stands out for me was when ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">This was about [X] years ago, back when I was ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">What happened was, ___. And then ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I remember feeling really ___ at the time.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Looking back, it taught me that ___.</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\"><strong>Past tense discipline.</strong> Pick past tense in sentence one and stay there for all 60 seconds. Mid-story tense slips are the #1 thing dragging a 7 down from a 9. Prepare 6 flexible real stories (a mistake, a success, a trip, a person who helped you, a hard decision, a first time) — almost any prompt bends to fit one of them.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"tpl\">\n      <h4>Task 3 — Describing a Scene</h4>\n      <div class=\"meta\">30s prep · 60s · describe only what you SEE</div>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">This picture shows ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">In the foreground, on the left, there's a man who's ___ing.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Next to him, two women are ___ing.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">In the background, I can see ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Overall, it looks like a ___ kind of day / a pretty busy scene.</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">Two mechanical wins here: (1) <strong>every verb in present continuous</strong> — is walking, are sitting, is holding. (2) Use a fixed sweep so you never run out: foreground → left → right → background → overall. Also describe people's clothing and age — free words that fill time accurately.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"tpl\">\n      <h4>Task 4 — Making Predictions</h4>\n      <div class=\"meta\">30s prep · 60s · same picture, now the future</div>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">In a few minutes, I think ___ is probably going to ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">It looks like ___ will ___ once ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">There's a good chance that ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I wouldn't be surprised if ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">And by the end of the day, they'll most likely ___.</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">Range of future forms is what scores here — mix <em>will</em>, <em>going to</em>, <em>might</em>, <em>probably</em>, <em>I expect</em>. Using \"will\" five times caps you at 7.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"tpl\">\n      <h4>Task 5 — Comparing and Persuading</h4>\n      <div class=\"meta\">60s prep · 60s · pick ONE option in the first 3 seconds</div>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I've looked at both, and I really think we should go with ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Compared to the other one, this one is ___er / more ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">The main reason is ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">On top of that, ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I know you might be worried about ___, but honestly ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">So what do you think — should we go for it?</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">Comparative grammar is being tested directly: <em>cheaper than, more convenient than, not as big as, the best option</em>. Force three comparatives in. And address the listener's objection — that one move separates 8 from 9.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"tpl\">\n      <h4>Task 6 — Dealing with a Difficult Situation</h4>\n      <div class=\"meta\">60s prep · 60s · hardest task; choose your listener and commit</div>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Hi ___, I'm really sorry, but I've got some bad news about ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">The situation is that ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I know this isn't what you were hoping for, and I do apologise.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">What I'd suggest is ___. Alternatively, we could ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Would either of those work for you?</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">The trap is switching listeners halfway (\"I'll tell my boss... and I'd tell you...\"). Decide in prep: one person, one whole minute. Politeness markers score points — <em>I'm afraid, would it be possible, I completely understand, I do apologise</em>.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"tpl\">\n      <h4>Task 7 — Expressing Opinions</h4>\n      <div class=\"meta\">30s prep · 90s · PREP structure, two reasons</div>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Personally, I strongly believe that ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">There are two main reasons I feel this way.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">First and most importantly, ___. For example, ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Second, ___. I've seen this myself when ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Now, some people would argue that ___, and I get that, but I'd say ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">So overall, that's why I think ___.</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">90 seconds needs <strong>two reasons with a concrete example each</strong>. The counter-argument sentence is the highest-scoring line in the whole task — never skip it. Vague reasons (\"it's better for people\") score 6; specific examples score 9.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"tpl\">\n      <h4>Task 8 — Describing an Unusual Situation</h4>\n      <div class=\"meta\">30s prep · 60s · describing a strange object to someone who can't see it</div>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I want to tell you about something pretty strange I came across.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">It's roughly the size of a ___, and it seems to be made of ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">The top part looks kind of like a ___, and at the bottom there's ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">It's a sort of dark green colour, with ___ on the side.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">If I had to guess, I'd say it's used for ___ — but I'm honestly not sure.</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">Comparison is the scoring device: <em>it's shaped like, it reminds me of, it's similar to, it's about as big as</em>. Give size, material, colour, shape, parts, and a guess at purpose — six things, ten seconds each.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>The recording loop (do this exactly)</h3>\n    <ul class=\"plain\">\n      <li><strong>1.</strong> Voice memo on. Answer the prompt cold, on the clock. No stopping, no restarting.</li>\n      <li><strong>2.</strong> Type out <em>exactly</em> what you said — every \"um\", every wrong verb, every restart. This is the step people skip and it's the step that works.</li>\n      <li><strong>3.</strong> Mark your own errors first. Then paste to me and ask: <em>\"CELPIP band for this speaking transcript, plus my top 3 repeating errors.\"</em></li>\n      <li><strong>4.</strong> Add each error to the log tab.</li>\n      <li><strong>5.</strong> Re-record the same prompt, corrected. Notice how much easier the second one feels — that feeling is the skill forming.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>",
"writing": "<div class=\"card warn\">\n    <h3>Writing 7 → 9: what actually changes</h3>\n    <p>A 7 covers the content but is repetitive and has recurring small errors. A 9 has <strong>varied sentence length</strong>, <strong>clean paragraphing</strong>, <strong>consistent tone</strong>, and errors so minor the reader doesn't notice. You do not need fancier vocabulary — you need fewer leaks and more sentence variety.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:8px;\"><strong>Word count:</strong> 150–200. Aim for 180. Under 150 is penalised; over 200 wastes proofreading time and adds errors.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <div class=\"tpl\">\n      <h4>Task 1 — Writing an Email</h4>\n      <div class=\"meta\">27 minutes · 150–200 words · read the tone cue carefully (friend = casual, manager/company = formal)</div>\n      <p><strong>Time budget:</strong> 3 min plan → 18 min write → 6 min proofread. <em>Protect the proofread.</em> That's where your 7 becomes a 9.</p>\n      <h3 style=\"margin-top:14px;\">Formal shape</h3>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Dear [Name / Sir or Madam],</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I am writing to ___ regarding ___.</span> — purpose in sentence one, always</p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">[Paragraph 2 — bullet point 1, expanded into 3 sentences with specifics]</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">[Paragraph 3 — bullet points 2 and 3, with the reason behind your request]</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I would greatly appreciate it if you could ___. Please let me know at your earliest convenience.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Sincerely,<br>Ivan</span></p>\n      <h3 style=\"margin-top:16px;\">Casual shape</h3>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Hi [Name],</span> · <span class=\"frame\">Hope you're doing well! I'm writing because ___.</span> · <span class=\"frame\">Anyway, let me know what you think. Talk soon,</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\"><strong>The three bullet points in the prompt are the grading rubric.</strong> Each one gets its own real paragraph with specific detail — a date, a number, a name. Missing or thin-covering one bullet caps you at 6 no matter how good the English is.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"tpl\">\n      <h4>Task 2 — Responding to Survey Questions</h4>\n      <div class=\"meta\">26 minutes · 150–200 words · pick a side immediately, never balance</div>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I would like to express my support for Option A.</span> — first sentence, no hedging</p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">There are two main reasons for my choice.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">First and foremost, ___. In my neighbourhood, for instance, ___. This would mean that ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Secondly, ___. From my own experience, ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I do recognise that Option B would ___; however, I believe the benefits of Option A outweigh this concern because ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">For these reasons, I strongly encourage the city to move forward with Option A.</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">Two reasons, each with a <strong>concrete invented example</strong> (a specific street, a number, a time of day — nobody is fact-checking you). The concession sentence + \"however\" is a reliable band-9 move. Sitting on the fence is the classic 6.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Your 6-minute proofread, in this order</h3>\n    <ul class=\"plain\">\n      <li><strong>Pass 1 — plurals &amp; articles.</strong> Read every noun. Does it need <em>a / the / -s</em>? This alone will catch most of your errors.</li>\n      <li><strong>Pass 2 — verbs.</strong> Every verb: right tense? Does it agree with its subject? <em>He go → he goes.</em></li>\n      <li><strong>Pass 3 — sentence length.</strong> Any sentence over 30 words? Split it. Three short sentences in a row? Join two. Variety is scored.</li>\n      <li><strong>Pass 4 — bullet check.</strong> Point at each bullet in the prompt and find where you covered it. If you can't point to it, it isn't there.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <div class=\"tip\">Practise proofreading as its own skill. Twice a week, take an old piece of your writing and give yourself 6 minutes to find errors — count how many you catch. That number should climb every week.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Sentence variety kit — steal these connectors</h3>\n    <table>\n      <tr><th>Instead of always</th><th>Rotate through</th></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">And / Also</td><td class=\"good\">In addition, · Furthermore, · On top of that, · What's more,</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">But</td><td class=\"good\">However, · That said, · Although ___, · While it's true that ___,</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">Because</td><td class=\"good\">since · as · due to the fact that · the reason being that</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">So</td><td class=\"good\">As a result, · Therefore, · Consequently, · This means that</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">I think</td><td class=\"good\">In my view, · I'd argue that · It seems to me that · From my perspective,</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">Very big</td><td class=\"good\">substantial · significant · considerable · enormous</td></tr>\n    </table>\n  </div>",
"work": "<div class=\"card warn\">\n    <h3>\"I barely understand what coworkers are saying\"</h3>\n    <p>With L9 you can understand English fine — what you're missing is <strong>fast casual speech, contractions, and idioms</strong>. Native speakers don't say \"What did you do?\", they say \"Whadja do?\". Test listening is recorded and clear; your coworkers are not.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:8px;\">The fix is <strong>connected speech</strong>, and shadowing unscripted conversation (not TED talks) is how you get it. Also: give yourself permission to say <span class=\"frame\">\"Sorry, could you say that again?\"</span> Native speakers say it to each other constantly. Nodding when you didn't catch it is what actually damages your reputation at work.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"grid2\">\n    <div class=\"card\">\n      <h3>Small talk — the 4 moves</h3>\n      <p style=\"font-size:14px;color:#5a5a5a;margin-bottom:10px;\">Small talk isn't a conversation, it's a 30-second ritual with a fixed shape. Learn the shape and you never have to improvise.</p>\n      <p><strong>1. Open</strong> — <span class=\"frame\">Morning! How's it going?</span> <span class=\"frame\">Hey, how was your weekend?</span> <span class=\"frame\">Busy week so far?</span></p>\n      <p><strong>2. React</strong> (never just \"oh ok\") — <span class=\"frame\">Oh nice!</span> <span class=\"frame\">That sounds fun.</span> <span class=\"frame\">Oh no, really?</span> <span class=\"frame\">Tell me about it.</span></p>\n      <p><strong>3. Add one small thing about yourself</strong> — <span class=\"frame\">Yeah, same here — I ended up just ___.</span> <span class=\"frame\">Funny you say that, I ___ over the weekend.</span></p>\n      <p><strong>4. Exit cleanly</strong> — <span class=\"frame\">Anyway, I should get back to it. Catch you later!</span> <span class=\"frame\">Alright, good chat — see you at standup.</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">Step 3 is the one you're probably skipping. If you only ask and react, the other person does all the work and it feels flat. One sentence about yourself is what turns it into a conversation.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"card\">\n      <h3>Standup &amp; status</h3>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Yesterday I mostly worked on ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I got ___ done, and I'm about halfway through ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Today I'm picking up ___.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">No blockers on my end.</span> / <span class=\"frame\">One thing — I'm blocked on ___ until ___ gets back to me.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">I might need a hand with ___ later, if anyone's free.</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">Write your standup out the night before for the first two weeks. Reading a prepared 20-second update confidently beats improvising a nervous 60-second one.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Explaining a technical problem — the 5-beat structure</h3>\n    <p style=\"font-size:14px;color:#5a5a5a;\">You said explaining reasons and telling the story is your weak spot. This is the fix. Five beats, always the same order, and you never lose the thread:</p>\n    <table>\n      <tr><th>Beat</th><th>Say</th></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>1. Headline first</strong></td><td><span class=\"frame\">So the short version is: ___ is broken / we're behind on ___.</span></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>2. Impact</strong></td><td><span class=\"frame\">What that means for us is ___.</span></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>3. Cause</strong></td><td><span class=\"frame\">The reason it's happening is ___. Basically, ___.</span></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>4. What you did</strong></td><td><span class=\"frame\">So far I've tried ___, and I've confirmed that ___.</span></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>5. The ask</strong></td><td><span class=\"frame\">What I need from you is ___.</span> / <span class=\"frame\">My plan is ___ — does that sound right to you?</span></td></tr>\n    </table>\n    <div class=\"tip\"><strong>Headline first</strong> is the habit to build. The instinct is to tell the story chronologically and arrive at the point last — that's what makes people look confused. Say the conclusion, then explain. Practise on every single problem you hit at work, out loud, alone, before you tell anyone.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"grid2\">\n    <div class=\"card\">\n      <h3>Meeting survival phrases</h3>\n      <p><strong>Buying time</strong> — <span class=\"frame\">That's a good question, let me think for a second.</span> <span class=\"frame\">Hmm, the way I'd put it is ___.</span></p>\n      <p><strong>Interrupting politely</strong> — <span class=\"frame\">Sorry, can I jump in here?</span> <span class=\"frame\">Quick thing before we move on —</span></p>\n      <p><strong>Disagreeing safely</strong> — <span class=\"frame\">I see it a bit differently, actually.</span> <span class=\"frame\">That makes sense, though my worry would be ___.</span> <span class=\"frame\">Have we considered ___?</span></p>\n      <p><strong>Checking understanding</strong> — <span class=\"frame\">Just so I'm following — you're saying ___, right?</span> <span class=\"frame\">Sorry, could you back up a bit?</span></p>\n      <p><strong>Not knowing</strong> — <span class=\"frame\">I'm not sure off the top of my head — let me check and get back to you.</span></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">That last one is a power move, not a weakness. Confident people say it constantly.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"card\">\n      <h3>Repair phrases (memorise these cold)</h3>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Sorry, could you say that again?</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Sorry, I missed the last bit.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">What do you mean by ___?</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Sorry, I'm not explaining this well — let me start over.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">How do you say it when ___? I'm blanking on the word.</span></p>\n      <p><span class=\"frame\">Bear with me, my English brain is buffering.</span> <em style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a8a8a;\">— light, self-aware, people like it</em></p>\n      <div class=\"tip\">These need to be <strong>automatic</strong>, not thought about. Say each one out loud 10 times today. Fluency isn't never getting stuck — it's getting unstuck smoothly.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Interview track — \"talk like an expert\"</h3>\n    <p style=\"font-size:14px;color:#5a5a5a;\">Sounding expert has almost nothing to do with vocabulary. It's <strong>structure</strong> (you answer in a shape, not a ramble), <strong>specificity</strong> (numbers and names, not \"some stuff\"), and <strong>pace</strong> (slower than you think, with pauses in the right places).</p>\n    <h3>Prepare 8 STAR stories, not 50 answers</h3>\n    <p style=\"font-size:14px;\">Almost every behavioural question maps to one of these. Write all 8, drill them until they're 90 seconds each:</p>\n    <ul class=\"plain\">\n      <li>A hard technical problem you solved</li>\n      <li>A time you disagreed with a teammate or manager</li>\n      <li>A mistake you made and what you changed after</li>\n      <li>A project you led or drove without authority</li>\n      <li>A time you had to learn something fast</li>\n      <li>A time you handled conflicting priorities or a tight deadline</li>\n      <li>A time you helped someone else succeed</li>\n      <li>Something you built you're genuinely proud of</li>\n    </ul>\n    <h3>The shape</h3>\n    <p><span class=\"frame\">S — At [company], we were dealing with ___.</span> <em style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a8a8a;\">(10 sec, no more)</em></p>\n    <p><span class=\"frame\">T — My job was to ___.</span> <em style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a8a8a;\">(5 sec)</em></p>\n    <p><span class=\"frame\">A — So what I did was ___. First I ___, then I ___. The tricky part was ___, so I ___.</span> <em style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a8a8a;\">(50 sec — the bulk, and always \"I\", not \"we\")</em></p>\n    <p><span class=\"frame\">R — In the end, ___. We cut it from X to Y, and the team ended up ___.</span> <em style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a8a8a;\">(15 sec, with a number)</em></p>\n    <h3>Expert-sounding framing</h3>\n    <p><span class=\"frame\">The tradeoff we were weighing was ___ versus ___.</span></p>\n    <p><span class=\"frame\">The thing most people get wrong about ___ is ___.</span></p>\n    <p><span class=\"frame\">It depends on ___ — if ___, I'd go with ___; if not, ___.</span></p>\n    <p><span class=\"frame\">In my experience, the bottleneck is usually ___, not ___.</span></p>\n    <p><span class=\"frame\">We deliberately chose ___, even though ___, because ___.</span></p>\n    <div class=\"tip\"><strong>Slow down and pause.</strong> Rushing is what reads as nervous and junior. A deliberate one-second pause before answering reads as thoughtful and senior — and it buys you time to build the sentence. Speed does not equal fluency.</div>\n  </div>",
"canto": "<div class=\"card warn\">\n    <h3>Your errors are systematic, not random</h3>\n    <p>You said your grammar is \"all wrong\". It isn't — it's <strong>wrong in about nine predictable ways</strong>, and every one of them traces back to a place where Cantonese and English are built differently. Random errors are hard to fix. Systematic ones are easy, because you can hunt one pattern at a time.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:8px;\">Read this once. Then don't study it — just use it to recognise your own mistakes faster when you transcribe.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>The nine Cantonese interference patterns</h3>\n    <table>\n      <tr><th style=\"width:20%\">Pattern</th><th style=\"width:34%\">What Cantonese does</th><th>What comes out in English</th></tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>1. No articles</strong></td>\n        <td>Cantonese has no <em>a</em> or <em>the</em>. 我send email = \"I send email\", complete and correct.</td>\n        <td><span class=\"bad\">I sent email to manager</span> → <span class=\"good\">I sent <u>an</u> email to <u>my</u> manager</span><br><em style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a8a8a\">Your single most frequent error. Fix this one first.</em></td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>2. Classifiers, not plurals</strong></td>\n        <td>三本書 (saam1 bun2 syu1) = \"three CL book\". The number already shows plural, so the noun never changes.</td>\n        <td><span class=\"bad\">three ticket, many problem</span> → <span class=\"good\">three ticket<u>s</u>, many problem<u>s</u></span></td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>3. Aspect, not tense</strong></td>\n        <td>Verbs never change form. Time comes from adverbs (琴日 = yesterday) and particles: 咗 zo2 done, 緊 gan2 in-progress, 過 gwo3 ever-experienced.</td>\n        <td><span class=\"bad\">Yesterday I go there and I see him</span> → <span class=\"good\">Yesterday I <u>went</u> there and <u>saw</u> him</span><br><em style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a8a8a\">This is why your stories drift tense — you're marking time once at the start, Cantonese-style, then stopping.</em></td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>4. No subject–verb agreement</strong></td>\n        <td>The verb is identical for I / you / he / they. There is nothing to agree with.</td>\n        <td><span class=\"bad\">He work here, it depend on</span> → <span class=\"good\">He work<u>s</u> here, it depend<u>s</u> on</span></td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>5. Topic-first word order</strong></td>\n        <td>Cantonese fronts the topic: 呢套戲我睇咗 = \"this movie, I watched already\".</td>\n        <td><span class=\"bad\">This bug I already fixed it</span> → <span class=\"good\">I've already fixed this bug</span><br><em style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a8a8a\">Sounds \"off\" to Canadians without them being able to say why.</em></td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>6. Adjectives are verbs</strong></td>\n        <td>佢好高 = \"he very tall\". No <em>is</em> needed — 高 already functions as the verb.</td>\n        <td><span class=\"bad\">The server very slow</span> → <span class=\"good\">The server <u>is</u> very slow</span><br><span class=\"bad\">I very like it</span> → <span class=\"good\">I <u>really</u> like it</span></td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>7. Serial clauses, no connectors</strong></td>\n        <td>Cantonese strings clauses together freely without <em>and / so / because</em>.</td>\n        <td><span class=\"bad\">Build failed, I check log, it was memory</span> → <span class=\"good\">The build failed, <u>so</u> I checked the logs. <u>It turned out to be</u> memory.</span><br><em style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a8a8a\">This is the run-on habit that makes your explanations hard to follow.</em></td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>8. Question particles &amp; A-not-A</strong></td>\n        <td>你去唔去? = \"you go-not-go?\" Questions are made with particles or repetition, never by moving words.</td>\n        <td><span class=\"bad\">I don't know what is the problem</span> → <span class=\"good\">I don't know what the problem <u>is</u></span><br><span class=\"bad\">You finish already or not?</span> → <span class=\"good\">Have you finished yet?</span></td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>9. No present perfect equivalent</strong></td>\n        <td>Duration is expressed flatly: 我做咗三年 = \"I do already three year\".</td>\n        <td><span class=\"bad\">I work here for 3 years</span> → <span class=\"good\">I<u>'ve been working</u> here for 3 years</span><br><em style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a8a8a\">High-value fix — this exact sentence comes up in every interview.</em></td>\n      </tr>\n    </table>\n    <div class=\"tip\"><strong>Do not try to fix all nine.</strong> Take #1 (articles) in Week 1–2, #3 (tense) in Week 3–4, and let the error log tell you which of the rest are actually yours. Some won't be.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>HK English that sounds wrong in Canada</h3>\n    <p style=\"font-size:14px;color:#5a5a5a;\">These are all perfectly normal in Hong Kong. In a Canadian office they mark you instantly — and some cause real confusion.</p>\n    <div class=\"grid2\">\n      <div>\n        <table>\n          <tr><th>HK</th><th>Canada</th></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Open / close the light</td><td class=\"good\">Turn on / turn off the light</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Add oil!</td><td class=\"good\">You got this / Keep it up</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">I very like it</td><td class=\"good\">I really like it</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Very delicious</td><td class=\"good\">Delicious / really good</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Handphone / mobile</td><td class=\"good\">Cell phone / my phone</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Play computer</td><td class=\"good\">Use my computer / game</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">My boss</td><td class=\"good\">My manager / my lead</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Aircon</td><td class=\"good\">AC / air conditioning</td></tr>\n        </table>\n      </div>\n      <div>\n        <table>\n          <tr><th>HK</th><th>Canada</th></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Until now it's fine</td><td class=\"good\">So far it's fine</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">...or not?</td><td class=\"good\">...right? / ...does that work?</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Same same</td><td class=\"good\">Pretty much the same</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Can lah / la</td><td class=\"good\">Yeah, that works</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">I will follow up the case</td><td class=\"good\">I'll follow up <u>on</u> it</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Do the needful</td><td class=\"good\">Take care of it</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Revert to me</td><td class=\"good\">Get back to me</td></tr>\n          <tr><td class=\"bad\">Discuss about it</td><td class=\"good\">Discuss it / talk about it</td></tr>\n        </table>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"tip\"><strong>\"Actually\" is your tell.</strong> HK speakers start sentences with <em>actually</em> constantly, because 其實 (kei4 sat6) works that way in Cantonese. In English, <em>actually</em> signals a correction — using it as a neutral opener sounds oddly contradictory. Count how many times you say it today. Replace most with nothing at all, or with <em>so</em>, <em>I mean</em>, <em>the thing is</em>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>The HK formality trap — this one costs you CELPIP marks</h3>\n    <p>HK business English is taught in a formal, officialese register: <em>Please be informed that... Kindly note... Herewith attached... We would like to express our sincere apologies for the inconvenience caused.</em></p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:8px;\">Two problems. First, <strong>CELPIP Writing Task 1 often asks for a casual email to a friend or neighbour</strong> — writing it in stiff business register is a tone mismatch and gets marked down even though every sentence is grammatical. Second, in a Canadian tech workplace this register reads as cold or sarcastic. Nobody writes \"kindly note\" in Slack.</p>\n    <table style=\"margin-top:12px;\">\n      <tr><th style=\"width:44%\">HK formal</th><th>Canadian workplace</th></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">Please be informed that the deployment has been completed.</td><td class=\"good\">Deploy's done.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">Kindly advise at your earliest convenience.</td><td class=\"good\">Let me know when you get a chance.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">I would like to express my apologies for the delay.</td><td class=\"good\">Sorry for the delay!</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">Please find attached the document for your perusal.</td><td class=\"good\">Attaching the doc — have a look when you can.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"bad\">I am writing to enquire regarding the status.</td><td class=\"good\">Just checking in on where this is at.</td></tr>\n    </table>\n    <div class=\"tip\">In CELPIP, read the prompt's relationship cue first and decide <strong>before</strong> you start: friend/neighbour → contractions, \"Hi\", exclamation marks, \"Anyway\". Company/manager/official → the formal template. Mixing the two registers in one email is a classic 6.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Cultural gaps that read as \"poor English\" but aren't</h3>\n    <ul class=\"plain\">\n      <li><strong>\"We\" instead of \"I\" in interviews.</strong> Chinese-influenced modesty makes you credit the team. Canadian interviewers read that as \"you didn't actually do it.\" In a STAR answer, the Action section is <strong>always \"I\"</strong> — I decided, I built, I pushed back. You can credit the team in one sentence at the end. This single change moves interview outcomes more than any grammar fix.</li>\n      <li><strong>Waiting to be asked.</strong> HK meeting culture waits for the senior person to invite you. Canadian meetings assume silence means you have nothing. Prepare one point before every meeting and say it in the first ten minutes — early, so it doesn't get harder.</li>\n      <li><strong>Disagreeing feels rude.</strong> It isn't, and never disagreeing makes you look disengaged rather than polite. <span class=\"frame\">I see it a bit differently</span> and <span class=\"frame\">my worry would be ___</span> are completely safe with a manager here.</li>\n      <li><strong>\"I don't know\" feels like losing face.</strong> In Canadian tech it's the opposite — <span class=\"frame\">I'm not sure, let me check and get back to you</span> reads as senior and honest. Nodding when you didn't catch something is the thing that actually damages your reputation.</li>\n      <li><strong>Small talk isn't small.</strong> It feels pointless and intrusive coming from HK office norms. Here it's the entire mechanism by which people decide they trust you. The weekend question is not nosy — it's the handshake.</li>\n      <li><strong>Self-deprecation lands differently.</strong> \"My English is very poor, sorry\" is normal humility in HK and reads in Canada as an actual warning label. Drop it. If you need to flag it: <span class=\"frame\">Bear with me, I'm still working on my English</span> — said once, lightly, then never again.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>",
"sound": "<div class=\"card warn\">\n    <h3>The insight that matters most on this whole page</h3>\n    <p><strong>Some of your \"grammar errors\" in speaking are pronunciation errors.</strong></p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:8px;\">Cantonese has no final consonant clusters, and its final stops (-p, -t, -k) are unreleased — you close your mouth but never let the air out. So when you say <em>three books</em>, the <em>-s</em> genuinely doesn't come out. When you say <em>I worked late</em>, the <em>-ed</em> vanishes. You know the rule. You applied the rule. The rater still hears <em>\"three book\"</em> and marks a grammar error.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:8px;\">This means a chunk of your Speaking 6 may be fixable <strong>without learning any grammar at all</strong> — just by physically finishing your words. It's also the fastest-moving item here: two weeks of daily drilling produces audible change.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:8px;\"><strong>Test it yourself today.</strong> Record: <em>\"She asked me for the sixth time last month, and I texted her the facts.\"</em> Play it back. Count how many endings actually survived.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Your five sound targets, in priority order</h3>\n    <table>\n      <tr><th style=\"width:20%\">Target</th><th style=\"width:32%\">What's happening</th><th>The drill</th></tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>1. Final -s and -ed</strong><br><em style=\"font-size:12px;color:#8a8a8a\">highest value</em></td>\n        <td>Dropped entirely. Kills plurals, third-person verbs, possessives, and all past tense at once.</td>\n        <td>Say these slowly, <strong>exaggerating the ending</strong> until it feels silly: <em>bookssss, worksss, needsss, walkeddd, fixeddd, askeddd</em>. Then in sentences: <em>He works. She asked. Three books. It depends.</em> 3 minutes daily.</td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>2. Consonant clusters</strong></td>\n        <td>Cantonese has none, so they collapse: <em>asked → ask, texts → tex, world → wor, months → mon</em>.</td>\n        <td>Hardest first: <em>asked, texts, months, sixths, strengths, twelfths, clothes, desks, risks</em>. Slow, every consonant audible, then speed up. Don't add a vowel (\"ask-ed\") — just release the cluster.</td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>3. Stress-timing (rhythm)</strong><br><em style=\"font-size:12px;color:#8a8a8a\">biggest effect on sounding natural</em></td>\n        <td>Cantonese gives every syllable equal length and a tone. English squashes unstressed syllables into a weak \"uh\" (schwa) and stretches the stressed ones. Equal-weight English sounds robotic and choppy.</td>\n        <td>Tap the table on the stressed syllable only: <em>I <strong>WENT</strong> to the <strong>STORE</strong> to buy some <strong>MILK</strong></em> — three taps, and everything between them gets crushed. \"to the\" becomes \"tuhthuh\". Do this with every shadowing clip.</td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>4. th / v / r</strong></td>\n        <td><em>th</em> → f or d (<em>three→free, this→dis</em>). <em>v</em> → w or f (<em>very→wery, have→haf</em>). <em>r</em> → w or l.</td>\n        <td><em>th</em>: tongue tip lightly between the teeth, blow. <em>think, three, both, month</em> / <em>this, that, they, other</em>.<br><em>v</em>: top teeth on bottom lip, <strong>voiced</strong> — feel the buzz. <em>very, video, have, five, believe</em>. Contrast pairs: <em>very/berry, vine/wine</em>.</td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td><strong>5. Intonation range</strong></td>\n        <td>Cantonese uses pitch to distinguish <em>words</em> (6 tones), so using pitch for <em>emotion and emphasis</em> feels unnatural. Result: flat delivery, which CELPIP raters score as limited range and coworkers read as disinterested.</td>\n        <td>Take one sentence — <em>\"I didn't say he stole the money.\"</em> Say it seven times, stressing a different word each time. Notice the meaning changes each time. Then deliberately over-do the pitch on your CELPIP recordings; it'll feel theatrical and sound normal.</td>\n      </tr>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Why you can't follow your coworkers — and the fix</h3>\n    <p>With Listening 9 your comprehension is fine. What defeats you is <strong>connected speech</strong>: natives don't say words separately, they blend and delete. HK schooling taught you the full, careful forms — your coworkers use none of them.</p>\n    <table>\n      <tr><th style=\"width:30%\">What you were taught</th><th style=\"width:30%\">What they actually say</th><th>Pattern</th></tr>\n      <tr><td>What are you doing?</td><td class=\"good\">Whatcha doin'?</td><td>Reduction + dropped g</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Did you eat yet?</td><td class=\"good\">Jeet yet?</td><td>Full collapse</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>I am going to check it out</td><td class=\"good\">I'm gonna checkidout</td><td>gonna + flapped t + linking</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Let me know</td><td class=\"good\">Lemme know</td><td>Reduction</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>I don't know</td><td class=\"good\">I dunno / Iunno</td><td>Reduction</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Could have been</td><td class=\"good\">Coulda been</td><td>have → uh</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Want to grab a coffee?</td><td class=\"good\">Wanna grabba coffee?</td><td>wanna + linking</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>It is not a big deal</td><td class=\"good\">'s not a big deal</td><td>Deletion</td></tr>\n    </table>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:12px;\"><strong>The fix is to speak them, not study them.</strong> You learn to hear a reduction by producing it. Use <em>gonna, wanna, gotta, kinda, lemme, dunno</em> in your own speech this week — it's fully standard spoken English, not slang, and it will make fast speech suddenly parseable.</p>\n    <div class=\"tip\"><strong>Change your shadowing material.</strong> TED talks and news are carefully articulated — good for Weeks 1–2, useless for this problem. From Week 3, shadow <em>unscripted conversation</em>: podcast interviews, two people interrupting each other, recorded meetings from your own company if you can get them. That's the speech you're actually failing to catch.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Your daily 10-minute sound block</h3>\n    <ul class=\"plain\">\n      <li><strong>Min 1–3 — endings.</strong> 20 words with -s / -ed, exaggerated. Then 5 sentences using them.</li>\n      <li><strong>Min 4–5 — clusters.</strong> The hard list: <em>asked, texts, months, desks, strengths, clothes</em>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Min 6–8 — rhythm.</strong> One sentence from today's shadow clip. Tap out the stress. Say it 10 times matching the native rhythm exactly.</li>\n      <li><strong>Min 9–10 — your own worst sound.</strong> Whichever of th / v / r your recordings show is worst. Minimal pairs, 20 reps.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <div class=\"tip\">Record yourself on day 1 and again on day 14 saying the same paragraph. Don't judge progress by feel — feel lies. The recordings won't.</div>\n  </div>",
"grammar": "<div class=\"card warn\">\n    <h3>Don't study grammar. Study <em>your</em> grammar.</h3>\n    <p>Working through a grammar book is a bad use of your 8 weeks. You have maybe 8–10 recurring errors that account for nearly all your lost points. Find them with the error log, then drill only those.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:8px;\">Below is the quick-reference version. The <em>Cantonese Traps</em> tab explains <strong>why</strong> you make each one — read that first, then use this table as the day-to-day lookup. Check both against your own transcripts and <strong>ignore anything that isn't actually yours.</strong></p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:8px;\"><strong>Before you blame grammar:</strong> if the error is a missing <em>-s</em> or <em>-ed</em> in <em>speaking</em>, check the Pronunciation tab first. You may be applying the rule correctly and just not finishing the word.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Quick reference</h3>\n    <table>\n      <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">Error</th><th style=\"width:38%\">Wrong → Right</th><th>The rule that fixes it</th></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Articles</strong></td><td><span class=\"bad\">I sent email to manager</span> → <span class=\"good\">I sent <u>an</u> email to <u>my</u> manager</span></td><td>Every singular countable noun needs a determiner: a / the / my / this. No exceptions.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Plural -s</strong></td><td><span class=\"bad\">three ticket</span> → <span class=\"good\">three ticket<u>s</u></span></td><td>If there's a number or \"many/several/a few\", the noun needs -s.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Third person -s</strong></td><td><span class=\"bad\">he work here</span> → <span class=\"good\">he work<u>s</u> here</span></td><td>he / she / it / the system / my boss → verb takes -s in present tense.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Tense drift</strong></td><td><span class=\"bad\">I went there and I see him</span> → <span class=\"good\">I went there and I <u>saw</u> him</span></td><td>Pick a tense at the start of a story and don't leave it.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Present perfect</strong></td><td><span class=\"bad\">I work here for 3 years</span> → <span class=\"good\">I<u>'ve been working</u> here for 3 years</span></td><td>Started in the past, still true now → have/has + past participle.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Uncountables</strong></td><td><span class=\"bad\">many informations / an advice</span> → <span class=\"good\">a lot of information / <u>a piece of</u> advice</span></td><td>Never plural: information, advice, feedback, research, equipment, software, work, progress.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Embedded questions</strong></td><td><span class=\"bad\">I don't know what is the problem</span> → <span class=\"good\">I don't know what <u>the problem is</u></span></td><td>Inside a sentence, question word order goes back to normal.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Run-ons</strong></td><td><span class=\"bad\">The build failed, I checked the logs, it was memory</span> → <span class=\"good\">The build failed<u>, so</u> I checked the logs<u>. It turned out to be</u> memory.</span></td><td>A comma can't join two full sentences. Use a period, or add and/but/so/because.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Prepositions</strong></td><td><span class=\"bad\">discuss about it · depend of · arrive to</span> → <span class=\"good\">discuss it · depend <u>on</u> · arrive <u>at</u></span></td><td>Learn as whole chunks, never as rules. Log every one you get wrong.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Word forms</strong></td><td><span class=\"bad\">it was success</span> → <span class=\"good\">it was <u>a success</u> / it was <u>successful</u></span></td><td>When you learn a word, learn all four forms: noun / verb / adjective / adverb.</td></tr>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>How to actually fix an error (not just notice it)</h3>\n    <ul class=\"plain\">\n      <li><strong>1. Catch it in the log</strong> with the wrong version and the right version side by side.</li>\n      <li><strong>2. Write 10 correct sentences</strong> using that pattern, about your real life. Not textbook sentences — your job, your weekend.</li>\n      <li><strong>3. Say all 10 out loud.</strong> Grammar you've only written doesn't survive into speech.</li>\n      <li><strong>4. Next day, pick 3 and say them again</strong> from memory. Then again 3 days later.</li>\n      <li><strong>5. Spot-check</strong> your next transcript specifically for that one error. When you go two weeks clean, retire it from the log.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <div class=\"tip\">One error at a time. Trying to fix five at once means fixing none — while you're watching articles you'll drop the plurals. Pick your worst one each week, hunt only that one.</div>\n  </div>"
};
